        "layout": "left",
        "text_alignment": "center",
        "title": "Skin’s immune system includes...",
        "text": "<p>... immune cells (Langerhans cells, keratinocytes), the microbiome, and signalling molecules — forming the first line of defense against external threats. Immunocosmetics gently interact with these structures via active ingredients that:strengthen the barrier, reduce hyperreactivity, support the microbiome, regulate inflammatory responses</p>",
        "button_label": "",
        "button_link": ""
      }
    },
    "text_rich_text_NFj8bP": {
      "type": "text--rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "richtext_tj7xMR": {
          "type": "richtext",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>In immunocosmetic care, <strong>lactobacilli </strong>are seen not only as prebiotics or microbiome modifiers, but as <strong>true modulators of innate skin immunity.</strong> Their activity occurs through interaction with epidermal cells (notably keratinocytes), induction of antimicrobial peptides, and modulation of pro-inflammatory signalling pathways.</p><p>This <strong>enables targeting critical immune mechanisms</strong> in sensitive skin:</p><ul><li>reduced colonization by pathogens (especially <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>)</li><li>stabilized microbiome</li><li>decreased neuro‑immune reactivity</li><li>restored barrier integrity</li></ul>",
            "text_size": 16
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "richtext_tj7xMR"
